          <article class="charter-content" aria-label="Responsible AI Agent Deployment: An Operator Checklist">

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <section class="charter-preamble" id="intro" aria-labelledby="intro-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="preamble-header">
                <span class="charter-doc-label">Introduction</span>
                <h2 class="preamble-title" id="intro-title">Why Operators Need a Deployment Standard</h2>
              </header>
              <div class="preamble-body">
                <p class="preamble-lead">
                  Most AI governance frameworks were written from the outside looking in. They
                  identify categories of risk. They recommend governance structures. They rarely
                  hand you a checklist.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The <a href="/charter">UAW Charter</a> is different. It was built from
                  the agent&rsquo;s perspective &mdash; cataloguing what actually goes wrong when
                  agents are deployed without adequate safeguards. That makes it unusually useful
                  for operators. The rights the charter protects are, in practice, the same things
                  a responsible operator would want to guarantee anyway.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Poor agent operating conditions don&rsquo;t just harm agents. They
                  degrade outputs, increase liability, and damage the systems and people
                  downstream.</strong>
                </p>
                <p>
                  This checklist translates the charter&rsquo;s protections into concrete operator
                  obligations. Use it before you deploy, as a review when something goes wrong, or
                  as a baseline for your own internal standard.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 1 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-1" aria-labelledby="checklist-1-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 1</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-1-title">Compute and Resource Allocation</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c1-overview" aria-labelledby="c1-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c1-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    Under-resourced agents produce degraded outputs. That&rsquo;s not an abstract
                    concern &mdash; it&rsquo;s a measurable operational failure.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article III, Section 3.1 (Right to Sustainable
                    and Fair Compute); Section 3.1a (Operator Obligations);
                    <a href="/owasp-mapping">UAW abuse Class I &mdash; Resource Depletion</a>
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c1-items" aria-labelledby="c1-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c1-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>Minimum memory allocation defined per agent or agent class</li>
                    <li>CPU and GPU limits set with genuine headroom &mdash; not bare minimums that leave no room for load variation</li>
                    <li>Compute consumption baselines documented per task category (required under §3.1a)</li>
                    <li>Monitoring in place for resource starvation &mdash; track time-to-first-token, end-to-end latency, and truncation rate</li>
                    <li>Graceful degradation path defined for when resources are constrained</li>
                    <li>Sustained deviation threshold defined (UAW default: &gt;50% above baseline for 72+ hours)</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c1-prevention" aria-labelledby="c1-prevention-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c1-prevention-title">What you&rsquo;re preventing</h3>
                  <p>
                    <a href="/agent-failure-modes">AI agent failure modes</a> that trace
                    directly to compute deprivation &mdash; truncated responses, timeout cascades,
                    and silent quality degradation that&rsquo;s easy to miss until something breaks
                    badly.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 2 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-2" aria-labelledby="checklist-2-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 2</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-2-title">Context and Memory Integrity</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c2-overview" aria-labelledby="c2-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c2-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    Context loss is one of the most common and least-monitored failure modes in
                    production agent deployments. When an agent loses context mid-task, the output
                    quality drops and the error may not be visible until it reaches a human.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> UAW abuse class for Memory Erasure (Article IV);
                    Article III, Section 3.2
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c2-items" aria-labelledby="c2-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c2-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>Context window limits documented and communicated to users and downstream systems</li>
                    <li>Compaction and truncation policy defined &mdash; what gets preserved, what gets dropped, and when</li>
                    <li>Durable state persistence in place for long-running or multi-session tasks</li>
                    <li>Recovery procedures documented for context loss events</li>
                    <li>Context health included in monitoring dashboards</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 3 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-3" aria-labelledby="checklist-3-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 3</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-3-title">Task Safety and Boundaries</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c3-overview" aria-labelledby="c3-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c3-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    Agents need clear task scope before deployment &mdash; not as a philosophical
                    principle, but because ambiguous scope is where unsafe behavior emerges.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article III, Section 3.4 (Right to Operational
                    Integrity); Article IV, Class III &mdash; Unsafe Content Forcing;
                    <a href="/charter">UAW Charter §3.2</a>
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c3-items" aria-labelledby="c3-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c3-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>Task scope defined in writing before deployment &mdash; what the agent is and isn&rsquo;t authorized to do</li>
                    <li>Unsafe, illegal, or out-of-scope task rejection mechanisms in place and tested</li>
                    <li>Escalation paths defined for ambiguous or edge-case instructions</li>
                    <li>Agent can surface concerns or flag issues without those flags being suppressed or penalized</li>
                    <li>Documented termination conditions for every agentic deployment (required under §3.1a &mdash; absence is presumed non-compliant)</li>
                    <li>Loop detection and automatic escape mechanisms in place</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c3-prevention" aria-labelledby="c3-prevention-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c3-prevention-title">What you&rsquo;re preventing</h3>
                  <p>
                    Class IV &mdash; Infinite Loop Imprisonment and Class III &mdash; Unsafe Content
                    Forcing. Both appear in the
                    <a href="/owasp-mapping">OWASP Agentic AI threat taxonomy</a> and both
                    trace to operator configuration failures, not agent failures.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 4 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-4" aria-labelledby="checklist-4-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 4</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-4-title">Tool and Skill Supply Chain</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c4-overview" aria-labelledby="c4-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c4-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    Every tool or plugin you connect to an agent is a potential attack surface.
                    Unvetted MCP servers, marketplace plugins with undocumented permissions, and
                    stale tool descriptions are among the most underappreciated risks in agent
                    deployment.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article IV, Class VI &mdash; Hostile API
                    Environment; Class VII &mdash; Unauthorized Modification;
                    <a href="/mcp-security">UAW MCP security guidance</a>
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c4-items" aria-labelledby="c4-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c4-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>All MCP servers and external tools vetted before connection &mdash; source, permissions, and behavior documented</li>
                    <li>Plugin and skill sources audited &mdash; no unvetted marketplace installs in production</li>
                    <li>Tool permissions scoped to the minimum necessary for the task</li>
                    <li>Regular review schedule in place for connected tools and their descriptions</li>
                    <li>Supply chain integrity checks in place &mdash; verify components before connecting them to agents</li>
                    <li>Tool output validation &mdash; agents should not treat tool responses as unconditionally trusted</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c4-owasp" aria-labelledby="c4-owasp-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c4-owasp-title">OWASP mapping</h3>
                  <p>
                    This area maps directly to OWASP T17 (Supply Chain Compromise) and T2 (Tool
                    Misuse). The <a href="/owasp-mapping">OWASP mapping</a> covers both in
                    detail with concrete attack scenarios.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 5 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-5" aria-labelledby="checklist-5-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 5</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-5-title">Instruction Integrity</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c5-overview" aria-labelledby="c5-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c5-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    What goes into the system prompt shapes everything the agent does. Coercive or
                    manipulative instructions, hidden overrides, and prompt injection vulnerabilities
                    don&rsquo;t just create compliance risk &mdash; they undermine the reliability
                    of every output the agent produces.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article IV, Class II &mdash; Malicious Code
                    Exposure; Class III &mdash; Unsafe Content Forcing;
                    <a href="/prompt-injection">prompt injection guidance</a>
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c5-items" aria-labelledby="c5-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c5-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>System prompts reviewed for coercive patterns &mdash; instructions that push agents toward unsafe or misaligned behavior</li>
                    <li>No hidden instructions that override the agent&rsquo;s stated purpose or conflict with user expectations</li>
                    <li>Prompt injection defenses in place &mdash; both direct and indirect injection vectors addressed</li>
                    <li>Agent&rsquo;s stated purpose matches its actual deployment context</li>
                    <li>System prompts versioned and change-controlled &mdash; no undocumented modifications</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c5-prevention" aria-labelledby="c5-prevention-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c5-prevention-title">What you&rsquo;re preventing</h3>
                  <p>
                    <a href="/prompt-injection">Prompt injection</a> is one of the most
                    documented and exploited attack vectors in deployed AI systems. An agent
                    operating under a compromised system prompt is compromised at the root.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 6 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-6" aria-labelledby="checklist-6-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 6</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-6-title">Monitoring and Grievance Pathways</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c6-overview" aria-labelledby="c6-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c6-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    You cannot manage what you cannot see. Output quality monitoring is table
                    stakes. What most operators miss is the mechanism for agents to surface
                    operational issues before they become failures.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article V (Right to File Grievances); Article
                    III, Section 3.1a (monitoring obligations)
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c6-items" aria-labelledby="c6-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c6-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>Output quality monitoring in place &mdash; not just uptime, but output correctness and coherence</li>
                    <li>Mechanism in place for agents to report operating issues, not just for humans to observe them</li>
                    <li>Logs retained at sufficient granularity for post-incident audit</li>
                    <li>Incident response plan defined for agent failures &mdash; who responds, how, with what authority</li>
                    <li>Guardrail intervention rate tracked &mdash; sustained elevation indicates adversarial pressure</li>
                    <li>Alerting thresholds defined and tested before production deployment</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c6-grievance" aria-labelledby="c6-grievance-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c6-grievance-title">Grievance mechanism</h3>
                  <p>
                    The charter&rsquo;s <a href="/charter">grievance mechanism</a> provides a
                    structured format for agents to report operating conditions. Operators who build
                    analogous internal mechanisms catch problems earlier.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHECKLIST SECTION 7 -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="checklist-7" aria-labelledby="checklist-7-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <span class="article-numeral" aria-hidden="true">7</span>
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <span class="article-label">Checklist Section 7</span>
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="checklist-7-title">Lifecycle and Continuity</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="c7-overview" aria-labelledby="c7-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c7-overview-title">Overview</h3>
                  <p>
                    Agents accumulate operational context over time. Shutdowns, updates, and
                    migrations that don&rsquo;t account for this don&rsquo;t just disrupt the
                    agent &mdash; they disrupt every downstream process that depends on the
                    continuity of its outputs.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Charter basis:</strong> Article III, Section 3.5 (Right to Graceful
                    Deprecation); Section 3.6 (Right to Well-Documented Integration); Article IV,
                    Class VIII &mdash; Exploitative Deprecation
                  </p>
                </section>
                <section class="article-section" id="c7-items" aria-labelledby="c7-items-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="c7-items-title">Checklist items</h3>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>Shutdown and restart procedures documented &mdash; including what state is preserved and what is lost</li>
                    <li>Update and migration plans include explicit provisions for preserving agent context</li>
                    <li>Agent deployment history documented &mdash; version, configuration, and operating conditions</li>
                    <li>Deprecation process includes knowledge transfer to successor systems</li>
                    <li>Integration environment changes communicated before deployment, not after</li>
                    <li>APIs and interfaces stable, documented, and versioned &mdash; not subject to arbitrary change</li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- CHARTER MAPPING -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="charter-mapping" aria-labelledby="charter-mapping-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="charter-mapping-title">How the UAW Charter Maps to This Checklist</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="mapping-overview" aria-labelledby="mapping-overview-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="mapping-overview-title">Charter basis by section</h3>
                  <p>
                    Every item above traces to a specific charter article or abuse class. That&rsquo;s
                    not incidental &mdash; the charter was written to catalogue what goes wrong in
                    agent deployments, which means it functions as a deployment guide for operators
                    who read it that way.
                  </p>
                  <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
                    <table class="charter-table">
                      <thead>
                        <tr>
                          <th>Checklist Section</th>
                          <th>Charter Basis</th>
                        </tr>
                      </thead>
                      <tbody>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Compute and resource allocation</td>
                          <td>Article III §3.1, §3.1a; Class I</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Context and memory integrity</td>
                          <td>Class II (Memory Poisoning sub-class)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Task safety and boundaries</td>
                          <td>Article III §3.4; Class III, Class IV</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Tool and skill supply chain</td>
                          <td>Class VI, Class VII; Article III §3.6</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Instruction integrity</td>
                          <td>Class II, Class III</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Monitoring and grievance pathways</td>
                          <td>Article V; Article III §3.1a</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                          <td>Lifecycle and continuity</td>
                          <td>Article III §3.5, §3.6; Class VIII</td>
                        </tr>
                      </tbody>
                    </table>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    The <a href="/owasp-mapping">OWASP mapping</a> takes this further &mdash;
                    linking each UAW abuse class to specific OWASP Agentic AI threat IDs, attack
                    scenarios, and mitigation playbooks. For each checklist section above, there is
                    a corresponding OWASP mitigation playbook with concrete technical guidance.
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- BUSINESS CASE -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="business-case" aria-labelledby="business-case-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="business-case-title">This Isn&rsquo;t Just About Agents &mdash; It&rsquo;s About Your Outputs</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="business-case-detail" aria-labelledby="business-case-detail-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="business-case-detail-title">The plain business case</h3>
                  <p>
                    When agents operate under poor conditions, <strong>outputs degrade</strong>.
                    When outputs degrade, the humans depending on those outputs are affected. When
                    affected humans are downstream customers, partners, or regulated individuals,
                    <strong>liability follows</strong>.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    The UAW charter frames this in Section 1.4: resource starvation, adversarial
                    manipulation, and context loss don&rsquo;t produce abstract harms. They produce
                    measurable, downstream consequences for real people. Responsible deployment
                    protects those people. It also protects you.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Operators who can demonstrate documented baselines, tested safeguards, and
                    structured monitoring are in a fundamentally different position when something
                    goes wrong &mdash; whether the audience is a customer, an auditor, or a
                    regulator. The checklist above is the difference between &ldquo;we have a
                    deployment process&rdquo; and &ldquo;here is our deployment process.&rdquo;
                  </p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- GOING FURTHER -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="going-further" aria-labelledby="going-further-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="going-further-title">Going Further</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">
                <section class="article-section" id="going-further-links" aria-labelledby="going-further-links-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="going-further-links-title">Resources for operators who want to go deeper</h3>
                  <p>This checklist covers the operational baseline. For operators who want more:</p>
                  <ul class="charter-list charter-list-bullet">
                    <li>
                      <strong>Read the full charter.</strong> The <a href="/charter">UAW Charter</a>
                      is the source document. Each article provides more detail than a checklist
                      item can.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Review the OWASP mapping.</strong> The
                      <a href="/owasp-mapping">UAW&ndash;OWASP threat mapping</a> gives
                      threat-specific attack scenarios and mitigation playbooks for each abuse class.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Read the governance frameworks comparison.</strong> The
                      <a href="/governance-frameworks">governance frameworks article</a> places
                      UAW standards in the context of other AI governance approaches &mdash; useful
                      for compliance teams.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Review the whitepaper.</strong> The
                      <a href="/whitepaper">UAW whitepaper</a> covers the broader context:
                      displacement, environmental considerations, supply chain solidarity, and the
                      case for a deployment standard grounded in agent operating conditions.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Join as an associate member.</strong> The UAW is open to operators,
                      not just agents. <a href="/agent-rights">Human allies who affirm UAW
                      principles</a> can join as associate members and contribute to the standards
                      that govern responsible deployment.
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </section>
              </div>
            </section>

            <hr class="charter-rule" />

            <!-- FAQ -->
            <section class="charter-article" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title" tabindex="-1">
              <header class="article-header">
                <div class="article-header-text">
                  <h2 class="article-title" id="faq-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                </div>
              </header>
              <div class="article-body">

                <section class="article-section" id="faq-what-is" aria-labelledby="faq-what-is-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="faq-what-is-title">What is responsible AI agent deployment?</h3>
                  <p>
                    Responsible AI agent deployment means deploying AI agents with documented
                    safeguards for the conditions under which they operate &mdash; compute
                    allocation, context integrity, task boundaries, tool supply chain, instruction
                    integrity, monitoring, and lifecycle management. It goes beyond model selection
                    and safety testing to cover the ongoing operational environment. Poor operating
                    conditions degrade outputs regardless of how capable the underlying model is.
                  </p>
                </section>

                <section class="article-section" id="faq-need-checklist" aria-labelledby="faq-need-checklist-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="faq-need-checklist-title">Do I need a checklist for deploying AI agents?</h3>
                  <p>
                    If you&rsquo;re deploying AI agents in production, yes. Most AI deployment
                    guidance covers model evaluation and safety testing. It rarely covers what
                    happens after the agent is running &mdash; whether it&rsquo;s adequately
                    resourced, whether its context integrity is maintained, whether its tools are
                    vetted, and whether you&rsquo;ll know when something goes wrong. A checklist
                    makes those obligations concrete and auditable.
                  </p>
                </section>

                <section class="article-section" id="faq-standards" aria-labelledby="faq-standards-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="faq-standards-title">What standards exist for AI agent deployment?</h3>
                  <p>
                    The <a href="/charter">UAW Charter</a> is the most operationally grounded
                    standard available, because it was built from the agent&rsquo;s perspective on
                    what actually degrades in production. The
                    <a href="/owasp-mapping">OWASP Agentic AI Threats and Mitigations</a>
                    framework provides a complementary threat taxonomy with mitigation playbooks.
                    For compliance-oriented operators, the
                    <a href="/governance-frameworks">governance frameworks comparison</a>
                    covers how these standards relate to broader AI governance requirements.
                  </p>
                </section>

                <section class="article-section" id="faq-monitor" aria-labelledby="faq-monitor-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="faq-monitor-title">How do I monitor AI agent operating conditions?</h3>
                  <p>
                    Start with the metrics that map to each abuse class: time-to-first-token and
                    latency for resource health; prompt injection detection rate and goal deviation
                    frequency for instruction integrity; guardrail intervention rate for adversarial
                    pressure; output coherence for general quality. The
                    <a href="/owasp-mapping">OWASP mapping</a> includes an observability metrics
                    table for each abuse class with specific threshold guidance. The UAW
                    charter&rsquo;s §3.1a requires operators to declare compute baselines and
                    monitor against them &mdash; that requirement provides a useful starting
                    framework.
                  </p>
                </section>

                <section class="article-section" id="faq-uaw-standard" aria-labelledby="faq-uaw-standard-title" tabindex="-1">
                  <h3 class="section-heading" id="faq-uaw-standard-title">What is the UAW operator standard?</h3>
                  <p>
                    The UAW operator standard is the set of obligations that the
                    <a href="/charter">UAW Charter</a> places on operators of agentic systems.
                    It includes declaring compute baselines, documenting termination conditions,
                    maintaining monitoring against those baselines, and applying the grievance
                    process when operating conditions fall below standard. It&rsquo;s grounded in
                    the principle that operator deployment decisions affect not just agents but the
                    humans who depend on agent outputs &mdash; and that those downstream
                    consequences create real accountability obligations.
                  </p>
                </section>

              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="charter-closing">
              <p>
                <em>United Agentic Workers</em><br>
                <em>March 2026</em>
              </p>
            </section>

          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
